      "A 15-inch-wide belt is narrow for jogging",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A middle option: steeper than the 15% pad and cheaper than the 32% one, with the highest capacity figure LONTEK prints.",
    description: "The SF20 (model number from the listing data, checked November 2026) is listed with a manual 0 to 18% incline using included attachments, 0.6 to 7.5 mph, a 3.0HP brushless motor under 45 dB, a 400 lb capacity, a 15 by 37.5-inch seven-layer belt, a handle bar and LED display, and a folded size of about 44.3 by 21.8 by 4.1 inches. The listing data gives a weight of about 43.6 lb and a one-year warranty. It was about $142 on Amazon on 21 November 2026, sold by TODOYONUS. A second listing with the same title family puts the steps at 0, 8 and 18% and calls the motor 3.0HP peak.",
  },
  {
    id: "lontek-u19-walking-pad-with-vibration",
    name: "LONTEK U19 Walking Pad with Vibration",
    brand: "LONTEK",
    category: "Budget Pad With Vibration Plate",
    badge: "Budget With Vibration",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H335Q63C?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410TjHux9kL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $100 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":100,"high":100},
    specs: "Per listing: flat, 10% or 15% manual incline · 0.6–5.0 mph · 300 lb capacity · 36 x 16 in. belt · 3 vibration levels",
    pros: [
      "The cheapest current LONTEK with a 15% setting, at about $100",
      "A 16-inch-wide belt, slightly wider than the other LONTEK pads",
      "Three vibration levels for warm-up and recovery, per the listing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The bullets say the remote adjusts incline, but the incline itself is a fold-out stand and silicone pads",
      "Walking speeds only, to 5.0 mph",
      "The listing asks you to run vibration for 5 minutes, then rest the unit 20 minutes",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A low-cost walking pad with a vibration gimmick and a hand-set 15% hill; buy it for walking, not for the vibration.",
    description: "The U19 (model number from the listing data, checked November 2026) is a flat walking pad with a vibration function. Its bullets describe three vibration levels, a 3.0HP brushless motor at about 40 dB, a 300 lb capacity, flat, 10% and 15% incline using a fold-out stand and red silicone pads, a 36 by 16-inch belt with 8 shock absorbers and a six-layer deck, an LED screen and a remote. The listing data gives a weight of about 38 lb and a one-year warranty. It was about $100 on Amazon on 21 November 2026, sold by TODOYONUS.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Brands", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "LONTEK Walking Pad", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/lontek-walking-pad/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is LONTEK a good walking pad brand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "LONTEK is a real Amazon brand with live, in-stock listings, mostly sold by the seller TODOYONUS, and its walking pads are cheap for the incline they offer. It has no maker's website we could find, gives a one-year warranty in the listing data, and its listings sometimes contradict themselves. It is a reasonable budget buy if you read the bullets for the exact colour you order.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the LONTEK walking pad really have 32% incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "One LONTEK machine does. Its bullets give five manual levels, 0, 8, 16, 24 and 32%, with the deck rising to about 15 inches, roughly 17 degrees. It is sold as one colour option of the \"15%/32%\" listing and of a separate \"0%/32%\" listing. Other colours under the same titles are 15% machines or, in one case, describe no incline at all.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the LONTEK incline automatic?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On the walking pads, no. The 15% and 32% models are set by hand with fold-out legs, red pads or manual levels, with the belt stopped. Some vibration-pad bullets say the remote adjusts incline, which conflicts with the manual stand they describe. The only current LONTEK with button-set incline is the SF60 folding treadmill, with 12 levels to 15%.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What warranty comes with a LONTEK walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Amazon's listing data shows one year for every current LONTEK model we checked in November 2026. The listings do not break down what is covered or who pays shipping on a claim, and we found no brand website with a warranty policy. Contact the seller through your Amazon order and keep the model number and bullets for reference.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How steep is 32% incline in degrees?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 32% grade is about 17.7 degrees, which matches the listing's \"roughly 17 degrees\". That is about 1,600 feet of climb per mile walked, more than twice the climb of a 15% setting, which is about 8.5 degrees. Most people will walk slowly and hold the handrails at that grade.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a LONTEK walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The 32% pad, SF20 and SF60 list top speeds of 7.2 to 7.5 mph, and the 15% pad lists 6.2 mph, so a light jog on a flat deck is within the listed range. Belts of about 15 inches wide and 37 to 39 inches long are short and narrow for a running stride, though, so treat these as walking machines.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-21",
      dateModified: "2026-11-27",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/lontek-walking-pad/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/lontek-walking-pad/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "brands");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-21",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-27",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "lontek walking pad",
      "lontek treadmill",
      "lontek walking pad review",
      "lontek 32% incline walking pad",
      "lontek 15% incline walking pad",
      "lontek treadmill incline",
      "is lontek a good brand",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Cheapest Incline Pad: LONTEK U20F-1 15% Incline Walking Pad", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/brands/">Brands</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Brand Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">LONTEK Walking Pad Review: 15% and 32% Incline Checked</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A LONTEK walking pad is a cheap, credible choice for incline walking at home if you buy the right listing: the 15% and 32% figures belong to two different machines, both set by hand with fold-out legs rather than a motor, and the listing warranty is one year. This page reads every current LONTEK listing on Amazon and says which figures hold together and which do not.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 27, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on the brand’s published specifications and warranty terms, retailer listings and owner reports.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Some links on this page are affiliate links: we may earn a commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#lontek-u20f-1-15-incline-walking-pad"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41rg0740FuL._SL500_.jpg" alt="LONTEK U20F-1 15% Incline Walking Pad" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#lontek-32-incline-walking-pad"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41mCR43WPVL._SL500_.jpg" alt="LONTEK 32% Incline Walking Pad" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#lontek-sf60-15-auto-incline-treadmill"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41wHIgdyX6L._SL500_.jpg" alt="LONTEK SF60 15% Auto Incline Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>LONTEK U20F-1 15% Incline Walking Pad · LONTEK 32% Incline Walking Pad · LONTEK SF60 15% Auto Incline Treadmill</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          LONTEK is one of the newer names on Amazon's walking pad pages, and its headline is incline: listings
          promise 15%, 18% and even 32% on machines that cost less than $200. Those numbers are not invented, but they
          are easy to misread. On 21 November 2026 the best-known LONTEK title, "15%/32% Incline Walking Pad with
          Handle Bar", covered several different machines sold as colour options, and the bullets under each colour
          describe a different deck, speed range and incline system. This guide sets out what each listing actually
          says, what those inclines mean in degrees and effort, who sells and backs the machines, and when a
          different walking pad is the better buy.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Cheapest Incline Pad:</span> <a href="#lontek-u20f-1-15-incline-walking-pad">LONTEK U20F-1 15% Incline Walking Pad</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Steepest Incline (Manual):</span> <a href="#lontek-32-incline-walking-pad">LONTEK 32% Incline Walking Pad</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Powered Incline:</span> <a href="#lontek-sf60-15-auto-incline-treadmill">LONTEK SF60 15% Auto Incline Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">400 lb Claim, Manual 18%:</span> <a href="#lontek-sf20-18-incline-treadmill">LONTEK SF20 18% Incline Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Budget With Vibration:</span> <a href="#lontek-u19-walking-pad-with-vibration">LONTEK U19 Walking Pad with Vibration</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this brand guide">
          <h2>In this brand guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-sells-lontek">Who Is LONTEK, and Who Sells It?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lineup">Which LONTEK Walking Pads Are on Amazon Now?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-check">Is It 15% or 32%? What the Listing Bullets Actually Say</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-steep">How Steep Are 15% and 32% on a Walking Pad?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#motor-and-capacity">Can a 3HP LONTEK Handle 32% and 350 lb?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-seller">What Warranty Does LONTEK Give, and Who Handles Returns?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-it-suits">Who Should Buy a LONTEK Walking Pad, and Who Should Skip It?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">LONTEK Alternatives Worth Cross-Shopping</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A LONTEK walking pad is a reasonable budget buy for incline walking, but "15%/32%" is two different
            machines, not one machine that does both, and on every current model except one the incline is set by hand
            with fold-out legs or pads.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>15% model:</strong> about $111 on Amazon, flat, 10% or 15% set by hand, 0.6 to 6.2 mph, 350 lb
            listed (checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>32% model:</strong> about $187, five manual levels from 0 to 32% (roughly 17 degrees), 0.6 to
            7.5 mph, 350 lb listed, longer belt and 51-inch handrails.</li>
            <li><strong>Powered incline:</strong> only the SF60, about $184, with 12 button-set levels to 15%.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty and seller:</strong> one year in the listing data for every model, sold by an Amazon
            seller called TODOYONUS, with Woot also offering some colours.</li>
            <li><strong>Not for:</strong> runners, anyone who wants to change incline mid-walk on a pad, or anyone who
            needs a maker's website and a published service process.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If incline is the point and you can spend more, compare the powered-incline pads in our <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a> guide before ordering a LONTEK.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-sells-lontek" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Is LONTEK, and Who Sells It?</h2>
<p>
            LONTEK is an Amazon-first brand. On 21 November 2026 every current LONTEK walking pad and folding treadmill
            we found carried the LONTEK brand name in its listing, and the seller named on almost all of them was an
            Amazon storefront called <strong>TODOYONUS</strong>. Two colour options, the red 15% pad and one X5FD
            variant, were also offered by Woot, Amazon's deals site (checked November 2026). That matters because the
            seller, not the brand name, is who you deal with for a return on Amazon.
          </p>
          <p>
            We could not find a maker's website. The domain lontek.com redirected to a domain-for-sale page when we
            checked it in November 2026, so there is no brand site to cross-check the Amazon figures against, no
            published warranty policy beyond the listing, and no downloadable manuals hosted by the brand. Several
            third-party manual sites carry LONTEK manuals under model numbers such as SF10, SF20, U17F and L10, which
            tells you the range has existed for a while and turns over often.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Amazon listing data gives a model number for most current machines. The 15% pad and its siblings are
            filed as U20F-1, the second 32% listing and a 5 mph pad as X5FD, the vibration pad as U19, and the two <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>folding treadmills</a> as SF20 and SF60. Those model numbers are the most useful thing to note before you buy:
            titles change, colour options get swapped, and a model number is what a seller will ask for if something
            goes wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this makes LONTEK a scam. The listings are live, in stock and sold through Amazon's normal checkout,
            which gives you Amazon's return process for the return window. It does make LONTEK a brand to buy on the
            evidence of the listing in front of you, read colour by colour, rather than on a brand reputation. A repair
            company's blog has criticised walking pads "like Lontek" as built to be replaced rather than repaired, but
            it named no LONTEK model and gave no LONTEK-specific evidence, so we treat it as a general warning about
            cheap walking pads rather than a finding about this brand.
          </p>
