        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is LONTEK a good walking pad brand?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              LONTEK is a real Amazon brand with live, in-stock listings, mostly sold by the seller TODOYONUS, and its walking pads are cheap for the incline they offer. It has no maker's website we could find, gives a one-year warranty in the listing data, and its listings sometimes contradict themselves. It is a reasonable budget buy if you read the bullets for the exact colour you order.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the LONTEK walking pad really have 32% incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One LONTEK machine does. Its bullets give five manual levels, 0, 8, 16, 24 and 32%, with the deck rising to about 15 inches, roughly 17 degrees. It is sold as one colour option of the "15%/32%" listing and of a separate "0%/32%" listing. Other colours under the same titles are 15% machines or, in one case, describe no incline at all.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the LONTEK incline automatic?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On the walking pads, no. The 15% and 32% models are set by hand with fold-out legs, red pads or manual levels, with the belt stopped. Some vibration-pad bullets say the remote adjusts incline, which conflicts with the manual stand they describe. The only current LONTEK with button-set incline is the SF60 folding treadmill, with 12 levels to 15%.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty comes with a LONTEK walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Amazon's listing data shows one year for every current LONTEK model we checked in November 2026. The listings do not break down what is covered or who pays shipping on a claim, and we found no brand website with a warranty policy. Contact the seller through your Amazon order and keep the model number and bullets for reference.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How steep is 32% incline in degrees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A 32% grade is about 17.7 degrees, which matches the listing's "roughly 17 degrees". That is about 1,600 feet of climb per mile walked, more than twice the climb of a 15% setting, which is about 8.5 degrees. Most people will walk slowly and hold the handrails at that grade.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a LONTEK walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The 32% pad, SF20 and SF60 list top speeds of 7.2 to 7.5 mph, and the 15% pad lists 6.2 mph, so a light jog on a flat deck is within the listed range. Belts of about 15 inches wide and 37 to 39 inches long are short and narrow for a running stride, though, so treat these as walking machines.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

